{
  "requirement": "Inspection preserves the requested path scope and the selected language for\noutlining and structural search.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject requires that inspection preserves (a) the requested path scope and (b) the selected language, for outlining and structural search. The sub-requirements `parse_snapshot` and `search_completeness` are refining requirements with their own implementations and tests, so I did not reassess them here.\n\n**Path scope**\n- `defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot` runs `search_text` with `SourceScope::under(\"src\")`. The recorded events include `list:src`, so the provider's `list` receives the requested scope. The test also asserts the match path and line.\n- `structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope` runs structural search with scope `src` at three limits. The `CachedParse` fixture records the scope each `list` call receives, and the test asserts it is `[\"src\",\"src\",\"src\"]`. It also asserts that the match path is `src/one.rs`, the selector is `fn[cached]`, and parsing used the provider's override.\n- `gitignore_is_shared_by_all_operations_with_or_without_a_git_repository` covers scoped text search on the real `Sources`. A prefix scope narrows results to `src/nested.txt` for `./src/../src`. An ignored-path scope and an escaping `..` scope are also checked. Scoped `list` is checked as well.\n- `scoped_inspection_skips_unrelated_directories_and_reports_relevant_ignore_errors` checks that a `src` scope lists only `src/one.txt` and does not touch the broken sibling directory. The default scope does fail on the broken sibling.\n\n**Selected language**\n- `list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search` covers the language obligation. A provider that declares language `rust` parses a resource named `generated` (no extension), and the outline resolves `fn[provided]`.\n- In the same test, a structural query for `rust` matches `call()` with selector `fn[provided]`. A query for `python` over the same source returns an empty result with `complete` true, so the selected language is respected. An unknown language is an error.\n- The same test checks that an explicit provider language beats the file extension: `file.py` with language `rust` still outlines as Rust. With no explicit language, `file.rs` is resolved by extension.\n\nTogether these scenarios assert that the scope and language reach the provider primitives, and that they change what is returned. A provider that dropped the scope or ignored the selected language would fail them. I found no uncovered obligation.",
  "references": "fb25fa326e584dedcc46b4cb5ffb943c498c41361dc844a88aafec157fd70a3f",
